## Synthetic validation: recovery scaling and the alarm's blind spot {#sec:synthetic-validation}

{{SYNTHETIC_VALIDATION_SCOPE}} To test the algebra across *many* ensembles — and
to probe the failure detector directly — we ran a
deterministic companion study on **synthetic judges** whose ground truth is set
by construction (`scripts/sim/synthetic_recovery_study.py`; no network, fully
seeded, reproducible). A trio with prevalence {{SIM_PREVALENCE}} and per-judge
`(a, b)` accuracies {{SIM_ACCS}} is sampled under error-independence, its votes
are fed through the **identical** `ErrorIndependentEvaluation` used on the LLM
votes, and the unsupervised recovery is scored against the sample truth. We
average {{SIM_N_TRIALS}} independent draws at each test size.

**Recovery error falls like sampling noise.** As the test grows from
`Q = {{SIM_Q_MIN}}` to `Q = {{SIM_Q_MAX}}`, the mean recovery MAE drops from
{{SIM_ACC_MAE_QMIN}} to {{SIM_ACC_MAE_QMAX}} ([@tbl:synthetic-convergence]). A log–log fit of error
against `Q` has slope **{{SIM_SLOPE}}** (R² = {{SIM_R2}}; the prevalence error
scales the same way, slope {{SIM_PREV_SLOPE}}) — close to the −0.5 of an
unbiased, sampling-noise-limited estimator. So the algebra
carries no persistent bias: the only thing between the unsupervised recovery and
the truth is finite-sample noise, and the real `{{BEST_MODEL}}` result (recovery MAE
{{REC_MAE}} at `Q = {{Q}}`) sits squarely on this curve rather than being a
fortunate one-off.

{{SIM_TABLE_CONVERGENCE}}

: Synthetic recovery error vs. test size, averaged over {{SIM_N_TRIALS}} error-independent ensembles per row. Every draw solved cleanly — no spurious alarms. {#tbl:synthetic-convergence}

![Unsupervised recovery error vs. test size `Q` on log–log axes, over {{SIM_N_TRIALS}} synthetic error-independent ensembles per point (accuracy MAE with ±1 sd error bars; prevalence error overlaid). The dotted line is a `1/√Q` reference; the fitted slope {{SIM_SLOPE}} (R² = {{SIM_R2}}) matches the −0.5 of a sampling-noise-limited estimator.](../output/figures/synthetic_convergence.png){#fig:synthetic-convergence width=90%}

**The scaling is not an artifact of one ensemble.** Repeating the convergence fit
across {{SIM_ROBUST_N}} deliberately different ensembles — varied prevalence
(0.50–0.70) and accuracy spread — keeps every fitted slope in a tight band
**[{{SIM_ROBUST_SLOPE_LO}}, {{SIM_ROBUST_SLOPE_HI}}]** around the ideal −0.5
([@tbl:synthetic-robustness]), so the sampling-noise-limited behaviour is a property of the algebra,
not of the particular judges we chose.

{{SIM_TABLE_ROBUSTNESS}}

: The `1/√Q` convergence slope is stable across diverse ensembles, each fitted over the same `Q` grid. All slopes cluster near the ideal −0.5. {#tbl:synthetic-robustness}

**The alarm is sound but has a blind spot.** The error-independent solve carries
a built-in failure detector: when the prevalence quadratic's discriminant
`D = 1 − 4c/a` goes negative the prevalence root is imaginary — an answer-key-free
signal that error-independence is violated. We characterised it by injecting two
kinds of dependence at `Q = {{SIM_CORR_Q}}` ([@tbl:synthetic-correlation],
[@fig:synthetic-alarm]). On genuinely
error-independent draws the alarm fired in **{{SIM_FALSE_ALARM}}** of trials — no
false positives. An **anti-correlated** judge pair (which drives a pairwise
vote-moment negative) tripped it in up to **{{SIM_ANTI_ALARM}}** of trials. But
positive **common-mode** correlation — all judges sharing a forced error, the
natural analogue of shared training data — never tripped it (**{{SIM_COMMON_ALARM}}**),
even as it silently worsened the recovery it failed to warn about (prevalence
error rising from {{SIM_INDEP_SILENT_ERR}} under independence to
{{SIM_COMMON_SILENT_ERR}} at the strongest common-mode setting).

{{SIM_TABLE_CORRELATION}}

: Alarm-firing rate by error structure at `Q = {{SIM_CORR_Q}}` ({{SIM_N_TRIALS}} trials each). `D` is the mean prevalence discriminant; the last column is the recovery error on the trials where the alarm stayed silent. {#tbl:synthetic-correlation}

![Left: the imaginary-prevalence alarm fires only under anti-correlation, never on independent or common-mode data. Right: the mechanism — as common-mode correlation strengthens, the discriminant `D` falls toward the `D = 0` alarm threshold but never crosses it, while the un-warned recovery error climbs.](../output/figures/synthetic_alarm.png){#fig:synthetic-alarm width=95%}

This sharpens the safety claim honestly: the imaginary-prevalence alarm is a
*sufficient*, never-false-positive detector of violated error-independence, but
it is **not necessary** — the most realistic LLM failure, positively-correlated
errors from shared pre-training, is exactly the regime it can miss. The
per-judge evaluability diagnostic ([@sec:evaluability-diagnostic]) and this alarm are complementary
screens, and neither is a substitute for a held-out check when correlated errors
are plausible.

## The two-solution tie-break needs clearly-better-than-random judges {#sec:tiebreak-boundary}

Error-independent evaluation returns two mirror-image solutions; we pick the one
with higher mean accuracy ([@sec:tie-break]), the standard better-than-random assumption. That
assumption has teeth, and a synthetic sweep shows exactly where it loses them. We
hold the trio symmetric (so mean per-label accuracy is the only varying factor),
fix prevalence {{SIM_TIEBREAK_PREV}} and `Q = {{SIM_TIEBREAK_Q}}`, and slide the
accuracy from strong toward random ([@tbl:tiebreak], [@fig:synthetic-tiebreak]).
For strong judges the
tie-break is essentially flawless — **0%** catastrophic mirror-selection down to
accuracy **{{SIM_TIEBREAK_SAFE_ACC}}** — but as the judges approach random the
mirror starts winning by chance and recovery inverts, reaching
**{{SIM_TIEBREAK_MAX_CAT}}** catastrophic selections near accuracy 0.5. The
practical rule: algebraic recovery is trustworthy only for an ensemble that is
*clearly* better than random; a barely-better-than-chance jury can be confidently
and silently inverted.

{{SIM_TABLE_TIEBREAK}}

: Catastrophic mirror-selection (prevalence error > 0.25) vs. mean judge accuracy at prevalence {{SIM_TIEBREAK_PREV}}, `Q = {{SIM_TIEBREAK_Q}}`, {{SIM_N_TRIALS}} trials each. {#tbl:tiebreak}

![The better-than-random tie-break is flawless for strong judges and inverts as the ensemble approaches random: catastrophic mirror-selection climbs from 0% to {{SIM_TIEBREAK_MAX_CAT}} and the mean prevalence error rises with it.](../output/figures/synthetic_tiebreak.png){#fig:synthetic-tiebreak width=90%}

## Error-independent vs. majority-voting: exactness vs. robustness {#sec:mv-ei-synthetic}

[@sec:mv-ei-real] reported that on `{{BEST_MODEL}}` the error-independent (EI) and
majority-voting (MV) evaluators returned the same recovery — explicitly *"a
property of this particular vote pattern, not a general theorem."* The synthetic
harness settles the general question by running **both** evaluators on the same
draws across a prevalence × accuracy grid (`Q = {{SIM_MVEI_Q}}`;
[@tbl:mv-ei], [@fig:synthetic-mv-vs-ei]). The answer is a clean trade-off,
not a winner:

- **EI is more accurate where its assumption holds.** For clearly-better-than-
  random judges the exact algebra beats majority voting — e.g. at prevalence
  {{SIM_MVEI_EI_PREV}}, accuracy {{SIM_MVEI_EI_ACC}}, EI's recovery MAE is
  {{SIM_MVEI_EI_EIMAE}} vs MV's {{SIM_MVEI_EI_MVMAE}} ({{SIM_MVEI_EI_RATIO}}×
  lower), and EI wins on {{SIM_MVEI_EI_WIN}} of draws. MV is *biased* — it assumes
  the majority vote is the truth — so it cannot reach the exact answer.
- **MV is more robust where EI's assumption fails.** In the tie-break danger zone
  of [@sec:tiebreak-boundary] (weak judges, here accuracy {{SIM_MVEI_MV_ACC}} at prevalence
  {{SIM_MVEI_MV_PREV}}), EI's mirror inverts and its MAE explodes to
  {{SIM_MVEI_MV_EIMAE}} while MV — which has no imaginary roots and no mirror —
  stays at {{SIM_MVEI_MV_MVMAE}}; EI wins only {{SIM_MVEI_MV_EIWIN}} of draws.

The practical guidance mirrors the diagnostics of [@sec:evaluability-diagnostic]
and [@sec:tiebreak-boundary]: prefer the exact
algebra for an ensemble that clears the evaluability bar and is clearly better
than random, but keep majority voting as the robust fallback when those
conditions are in doubt — and report both, as we do per run.

{{SIM_TABLE_MVEI}}

: Error-independent vs. majority-voting recovery MAE across a prevalence × accuracy grid (`Q = {{SIM_MVEI_Q}}`, {{SIM_N_TRIALS}} trials per cell). "EI wins" is the fraction of draws where the exact algebra's MAE was lower. {#tbl:mv-ei}

![Recovery MAE of the error-independent algebra vs. majority-voting as judge accuracy falls from strong to near-random, one panel per prevalence. EI is lower while judges are strong, then crosses above and diverges as its tie-break inverts; MV stays bounded throughout.](../output/figures/synthetic_mv_vs_ei.png){#fig:synthetic-mv-vs-ei width=95%}

